; The shape nobody wrote: `T` is bound to `&String` by the first argument, so
; the second parameter's `&T` is `&&String` and no value has that type. The
; refusal is the one a spelled `&&String` gets, at the argument that asked for
; it, rather than a mismatch naming a type the reader cannot have meant.
(fn pair (T) ((first T) (second &T)) -> i64
  0)

(fn main () -> i32
  (let text "hello")
  (let other "there")
  (pair &text &other)
  0)
